Chapter: LiteralAlloc
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 Does evaluating a Text LITERAL allocate, once per evaluation?

 The question is not academic and it did not start as a cost-model question.
 `&` on Text extends the left operand IN PLACE when that operand is the
 topmost allocation (`emit-str-concat-prologue`, `X86_64TextHelpers.codex`
 160-182, compares `left_base + aligned(len(left))` against the allocation
 frontier and only copies when they differ). So an accumulator appended to in
 a loop is LINEAR while it stays topmost. `p-append-text` in
 `accumulator-corpus` is `acc & "x"` with nothing else in its body, which
 should keep `acc` topmost, and it measured QUADRATIC at x12.7. Something in
 that loop allocates between the appends. The literal is the only candidate.

 Four arms, each run at n and 4n, bytes retained across the call from
 `__heap-save`, the same instrument the corpus uses.

Section: The Instrument

  keep : List Integer -> Integer
  keep (xs) = list-length xs

  keep-text : Text -> Integer
  keep-text (t) = text-length t

  ratio-tenths : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  ratio-tenths (at-n) (at-4n) =
    if at-n <= 0 then 0
    else (at-4n * 10) / at-n

  show-tenths : Integer -> Text
  show-tenths (t) = show (t / 10) & "." & show (t - (t / 10) * 10)

  row : Text, Integer, Integer -> Text
  row (name) (at-n) (at-4n) =
    name & " n=" & show at-n & " 4n=" & show at-4n
      & " x" & show-tenths (ratio-tenths at-n at-4n)

Section: Arms

 1. THE DIRECT QUESTION. No Text accumulator at all: evaluate the literal
    once per iteration and fold its length into an Integer. Bytes retained
    here are the literal's own cost and nothing else. Zero means a literal is
    a constant; growing with n means every evaluation materialises one.

  lit-eval : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  lit-eval (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else lit-eval n (i + 1) (acc + text-length "x")

 2. `p-append-text`'s exact shape, repeated here so both arms sit in one
    program and one allocator.

  append-literal : Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  append-literal (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else append-literal n (i + 1) (acc & "x")

 3. THE DISCRIMINATOR. The same append with the piece HOISTED into a
    parameter, evaluated once before the loop. If a literal allocates per
    evaluation, arm 2 is quadratic and this one is linear, and the difference
    between them is the whole finding. If both are quadratic the literal is
    not the cause and the topmost account is wrong about this shape.

  append-hoisted : Integer, Integer, Text, Text -> Text
  append-hoisted (n) (i) (acc) (piece) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else append-hoisted n (i + 1) (acc & piece) piece

 4. CONTROL. The same recursion doing no appending, so the loop's own cost is
    visible and arms 2 and 3 are read against it rather than against zero.

  no-append : Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  no-append (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else no-append n (i + 1) acc

Section: Measurement

  m-lit-eval : Integer -> Integer
  m-lit-eval (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let r = lit-eval n 0 0
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-append-literal : Integer -> Integer
  m-append-literal (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let r = keep-text (append-literal n 0 "")
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-append-hoisted : Integer -> Integer
  m-append-hoisted (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let r = keep-text (append-hoisted n 0 "" "x")
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-no-append : Integer -> Integer
  m-no-append (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let r = keep-text (no-append n 0 "")
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

Section: Entry

  base : Integer = 64

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (row "lit-eval       " (m-lit-eval base) (m-lit-eval (base * 4)))
    print-line-uni (row "append-literal " (m-append-literal base) (m-append-literal (base * 4)))
    print-line-uni (row "append-hoisted " (m-append-hoisted base) (m-append-hoisted (base * 4)))
    print-line-uni (row "no-append      " (m-no-append base) (m-no-append (base * 4)))
  end
